00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.510
So if you always asked yourself

00:01:50.510 --> 00:01:52.160
what this really is all about,

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:54.650
stay here and just keep learning.

00:01:54.650 --> 00:01:56.550
We would like to start to discuss with you

00:01:56.550 --> 00:01:59.233
what blockchains are
and why they are needed.

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:03.740
And let's begin with a simple
definition from Wikipedia.

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:05.440
What is a blockchain?

00:02:05.440 --> 00:02:09.000
A blockchain is a continuously
growing list of records

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:11.960
called blocks which are linked and secured

00:02:11.960 --> 00:02:13.483
using cryptography.

00:02:14.470 --> 00:02:17.400
Let's go over the key
parts of this definition.

00:02:17.400 --> 00:02:19.900
The blockchain is what
we are talking about

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:24.010
and it is a continuously
growing list of record.

00:02:24.010 --> 00:02:28.000
So this means the individual
blocks of the blockchain

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:32.690
contain records, contain
some kind of transaction data

00:02:32.690 --> 00:02:34.650
that are stored in individual blocks

00:02:34.650 --> 00:02:37.103
and individual components of these blocks.

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:40.840
And these components,
these individual blocks

00:02:40.840 --> 00:02:44.410
are linked together using cryptography.

00:02:44.410 --> 00:02:48.660
So using some mechanisms
that secure these blocks

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.420
to each other and make sure

00:02:50.420 --> 00:02:52.253
that they cannot be tampered with.

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:56.640
Let's have a bit more
visual representation

00:02:56.640 --> 00:02:58.580
of what we just discussed.

00:02:58.580 --> 00:03:01.180
This would be a very small
and simple blockchain

00:03:01.180 --> 00:03:03.570
consisting of five blocks.

00:03:03.570 --> 00:03:05.510
And the first thing that you're noticing

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:09.320
is that in every block, there
can be data secured into it.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:12.940
This is this list of records
we just talked about.

00:03:12.940 --> 00:03:15.330
So first, we start with the first block.

00:03:15.330 --> 00:03:17.730
Then some kind of data is created,

00:03:17.730 --> 00:03:19.390
some transactions are made,

00:03:19.390 --> 00:03:21.940
and the first block is
filled up with data.

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:23.840
And once the first block is full,

00:03:23.840 --> 00:03:26.300
then the second block is opened and again,

00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:29.480
we write data into this
block until it is full.

00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:32.500
And then we add a new block and so on.

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:33.600
And indeed, something like Bitcoin

00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:35.870
works in a very similar way.

00:03:35.870 --> 00:03:39.320
The block size, so the
size of individual blocks

00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:43.500
in the Bitcoin cryptocurrency
is one megabyte.

00:03:43.500 --> 00:03:46.200
So there is the case that after

00:03:46.200 --> 00:03:49.470
every one megabyte of transaction data

00:03:49.470 --> 00:03:52.060
a new block gets added to the blockchain.

00:03:53.410 --> 00:03:55.650
And what you see here is
also that these blocks

00:03:55.650 --> 00:03:57.480
are linked to each other.

00:03:57.480 --> 00:04:00.610
There's a cryptographic
link between these blocks.

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.100
And the link that is used in Bitcoin

00:04:03.100 --> 00:04:04.510
and most other blockchains

00:04:04.510 --> 00:04:07.750
is something called a hash function.

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:10.050
And in the next lecture, we will discuss

00:04:10.050 --> 00:04:12.740
in a bit more detail
what hash functions are,

00:04:12.740 --> 00:04:15.200
how they are used, and why they help

00:04:16.484 --> 00:04:19.300
to secure the blockchain
and make them tamper-proof,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:22.710
prevent other people from changing data

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.403
in a way that should not happen.

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.280
So why are they needed?

00:04:28.280 --> 00:04:31.610
Why do we even need
blockchain in the first place?

00:04:31.610 --> 00:04:34.000
And probably you could
come up with a whole lot,

00:04:34.000 --> 00:04:36.090
list of reasons, and one thing

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.210
that is very frequently
discussed in the literature

00:04:39.210 --> 00:04:43.410
is a thing called the
double-spend problem.

00:04:43.410 --> 00:04:45.230
So the problem that assets,

00:04:45.230 --> 00:04:48.570
that cryptocurrencies could be spent twice

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:52.750
if you use them in some
kind of digital way.

00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:54.580
And kind of the intuition of this problem

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:59.580
is just to think about Bitcoin
or any other cryptocurrency

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:01.910
as a file that you have on the PC.

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:04.850
So imagine you would have
some kind of file on your PC

00:05:04.850 --> 00:05:09.420
and it says the owner of
this file owns one Bitcoin.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:11.140
And the fact that you own this file

00:05:11.140 --> 00:05:13.580
or have this file on your computer

00:05:13.580 --> 00:05:16.693
kind of is proof that you
actually do own this one Bitcoin.

00:05:17.730 --> 00:05:20.110
Now if you would like to
buy something with Bitcoin

00:05:20.110 --> 00:05:22.850
or with whatever cryptocurrency
this is representing,

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:24.460
you would need to take this file

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.720
and send it to another person.

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:29.640
And now this other person
would have this file

00:05:29.640 --> 00:05:32.650
and would kind of now be
able to show that the person

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.010
owns a certain amount of cryptocurrency.

00:05:36.010 --> 00:05:39.070
So let's imagine person
A wants to transfer

00:05:39.070 --> 00:05:42.620
cryptocurrency in the value
of 100 US dollar to person B.

00:05:42.620 --> 00:05:45.423
Person A would send this file to person B.

00:05:46.350 --> 00:05:48.500
Of course, what is the problem now?

00:05:48.500 --> 00:05:51.380
It is inherent in the
nature of cryptographic

00:05:51.380 --> 00:05:54.700
or kind of digital goods
that they can be copied.

00:05:54.700 --> 00:05:58.780
If you have a file that
says the owner owns $100

00:05:58.780 --> 00:06:00.783
or whatever cryptocurrency
you're talking about,

00:06:00.783 --> 00:06:03.310
then you can easily just copy this file

00:06:03.310 --> 00:06:05.260
and send it to another person this way.

00:06:06.280 --> 00:06:09.940
So what can be done in
order to prevent person A

00:06:09.940 --> 00:06:14.220
to send a certain amount of
cryptocurrency to one person

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:17.000
but then send the same amount
to another person as well?

00:06:17.000 --> 00:06:20.060
And this problem is called
the double-spend problem

00:06:20.060 --> 00:06:24.030
and it is the exact reason
why before Bitcoin invented

00:06:24.030 --> 00:06:26.930
or kind of came up with
this blockchain concept,

00:06:26.930 --> 00:06:28.060
it wasn't really possible

00:06:28.060 --> 00:06:33.060
to have a decentralized
system of a cryptocurrency.

00:06:33.220 --> 00:06:36.450
Before it always required
a centralized system,

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.280
something like a bank
that kept all the records

00:06:39.280 --> 00:06:42.210
and was able to look into
all the different accounts

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:45.220
and was able to really see
whether the person was eligible

00:06:45.220 --> 00:06:47.720
to transfer the same amount
of money again or not.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:52.240
And blockchains can solve
the double-spend problem

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.400
because they provide a tamper-proof ledger

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.370
that is transparent and for
everybody open to check,

00:06:58.370 --> 00:07:01.430
and kind of enables the
mechanisms that are in place

00:07:01.430 --> 00:07:03.880
in order to prevent one person

00:07:03.880 --> 00:07:07.160
to double-spending a certain
amount of cryptocurrency

00:07:07.160 --> 00:07:09.000
or a certain amount of assets

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:10.973
with different types of other person.

00:07:15.180 --> 00:07:18.230
And to bid on that, you can
come up with a whole lot

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:21.280
of very tangible and
very relevant advantages

00:07:21.280 --> 00:07:24.110
that blockchains have over
more centralized ledgers

00:07:24.110 --> 00:07:26.870
like the ledger that a bank would have.

00:07:26.870 --> 00:07:28.110
And to go over them with you,

00:07:28.110 --> 00:07:30.776
probably the most relevant ones, first,

00:07:30.776 --> 00:07:32.970
blockchains are transparent.

00:07:32.970 --> 00:07:36.590
At least most blockchains that
you hear about are public,

00:07:36.590 --> 00:07:38.220
especially a blockchain like Bitcoin.

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:40.860
You can use a blockchain explorer

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:42.828
to look into every single block

00:07:42.828 --> 00:07:46.060
that has ever been created
in the Bitcoin blockchain.

00:07:46.060 --> 00:07:46.930
And you can look it up,

00:07:46.930 --> 00:07:49.030
you can look at all the transaction data.

00:07:49.030 --> 00:07:51.100
It's just there for you to see.

00:07:51.100 --> 00:07:54.060
You can see which transactions
were going through

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:56.350
one bitcoin address in the past.

00:07:56.350 --> 00:07:58.500
You can really look up
every single transaction

00:07:58.500 --> 00:08:00.390
with the amounts of kind of coins

00:08:00.390 --> 00:08:03.600
that were transferred
that exists out there.

00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.780
Second, it is trustless.

00:08:05.780 --> 00:08:07.930
Due to the nature of the blockchain,

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:11.440
it does not require any
centralized institution

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:12.870
that you need to trust

00:08:12.870 --> 00:08:15.810
in order to be able to
participate in the system.

00:08:15.810 --> 00:08:19.110
Even if you want to transact
values with the person

00:08:19.110 --> 00:08:21.150
that you inherently do not trust,

00:08:21.150 --> 00:08:23.600
you do not really want
to kind of be dependent

00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.010
that this person fulfills
his word, keeps this word,

00:08:27.010 --> 00:08:28.900
you can still use the blockchain.

00:08:28.900 --> 00:08:30.930
Because the blockchain makes sure

00:08:30.930 --> 00:08:32.990
that whatever agreement you came to,

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:34.860
whatever transaction you both filed

00:08:34.860 --> 00:08:36.840
or whatever transaction you made,

00:08:36.840 --> 00:08:39.450
this transaction actually will be executed

00:08:39.450 --> 00:08:42.303
and the other person cannot
do anything to take it back.

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:46.190
Next, it is decentralized.

00:08:46.190 --> 00:08:49.910
There is no such thing as
a centralized institution

00:08:49.910 --> 00:08:52.230
that kind of can go to your accounts

00:08:52.230 --> 00:08:55.460
and can just wipe your account,
take the money away from you

00:08:55.460 --> 00:08:58.160
or maybe even change
the value of your money.

00:08:58.160 --> 00:09:02.170
This is not possible because
of the decentralized nature

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:03.970
of most of blockchains like Bitcoin.

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:07.640
And finally, and of course
this is probably a summary

00:09:07.640 --> 00:09:10.410
of all these other points, it is secure.

00:09:10.410 --> 00:09:14.690
Due to kind of state of the
art cryptographic mechanisms

00:09:14.690 --> 00:09:16.660
that are implemented into blockchains,

00:09:16.660 --> 00:09:19.290
blockchains are believed
to be secure, and indeed,

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:23.990
the Bitcoin blockchain since
its inception since early 2009,

00:09:25.100 --> 00:09:27.960
it was never hacked, not even once.

00:09:27.960 --> 00:09:29.640
And kind of this is what I would say

00:09:29.640 --> 00:09:32.890
a pretty strong argument
because I don't think

00:09:32.890 --> 00:09:35.150
that there's any other computer system

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.510
that wasn't hacked for
such a long amount of time

00:09:37.510 --> 00:09:39.080
which was kind of connected and live

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:41.640
and active on the Internet.

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:43.630
So blockchains can be very practical.

00:09:43.630 --> 00:09:45.670
They have lots of use cases.

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:48.890
And we just discussed the
cryptocurrency use case

00:09:48.890 --> 00:09:50.390
a minute ago.

00:09:50.390 --> 00:09:52.030
And due to this practicality,

00:09:52.030 --> 00:09:55.660
they have the potential to
disrupt entire industries.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:57.700
And we will later have a section

00:09:57.700 --> 00:09:59.810
that focuses on exactly this point

00:09:59.810 --> 00:10:03.110
and builds on it and
discusses what use cases

00:10:03.110 --> 00:10:05.810
there exist with blockchains
and how industries

00:10:05.810 --> 00:10:07.073
can be affected by them.

00:10:07.960 --> 00:10:09.580
Now, in the next lecture,

00:10:09.580 --> 00:10:12.220
I will go into a bit more technical layer

00:10:12.220 --> 00:10:14.720
and try to give you a technical intuition

00:10:14.720 --> 00:10:17.100
of how blockchains work.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:19.040
In this lecture, I would like to give you

00:10:19.040 --> 00:10:22.590
a technical intuition
into how blockchains work.

00:10:22.590 --> 00:10:24.900
For that, I will use this website,

00:10:24.900 --> 00:10:27.870
it's anders.com/blockchain.

00:10:27.870 --> 00:10:30.410
I did not create this website myself,

00:10:30.410 --> 00:10:33.300
but it was created by Anders Brownworth,

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:35.980
so full credits for that to him.

00:10:35.980 --> 00:10:39.340
You can check out his site
here, it's andersbrownworth.com

00:10:39.340 --> 00:10:41.643
and also follow him here on social media.

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:45.320
And Anders also created these videos

00:10:45.320 --> 00:10:47.130
which will cover lots of the things

00:10:47.130 --> 00:10:49.400
that I will discuss now with you as well.

00:10:49.400 --> 00:10:51.623
So feel free to check these out as well.

00:10:53.150 --> 00:10:54.253
But let's get started.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:59.110
So the fundamental building
block of blockchain

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:01.550
is something that is
called a hash function.

00:11:01.550 --> 00:11:04.320
And here we see an example
of a hash function.

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:07.377
More precisely, this is a
hash function called SHA256.

00:11:09.850 --> 00:11:12.910
SHA stands for Secure Hash Algorithm.

00:11:12.910 --> 00:11:16.110
And what a hash function is
is that it takes an input,

00:11:16.110 --> 00:11:17.350
and this is now the data here.

00:11:17.350 --> 00:11:19.300
Currently it has nothing as an input,

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:22.780
and then it transforms
the input into an output.

00:11:22.780 --> 00:11:26.300
And the output is a hash
function which is a string

00:11:26.300 --> 00:11:29.330
of numbers and characters
that you see here.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:31.683
So whenever I type something in here,

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.130
you see that the hash
function as an output changed.

00:11:36.220 --> 00:11:38.560
And I can type whatever I want

00:11:38.560 --> 00:11:42.250
and the hash function changed accordingly.

00:11:42.250 --> 00:11:46.540
And interestingly enough is
if you now kind of delete this

00:11:46.540 --> 00:11:48.570
and insert the very same thing,

00:11:48.570 --> 00:11:51.270
the output will always be
the same based on the input.

00:11:51.270 --> 00:11:53.563
So let's look at this,
it starts with F096,

00:11:55.056 --> 00:11:57.100
so if I take this out, if we go back,

00:11:57.100 --> 00:11:59.630
and once I insert the very same string

00:11:59.630 --> 00:12:02.280
it will give me the same
output of the hash function.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:07.540
And now I can type in this
input field whatever I like.

00:12:07.540 --> 00:12:09.460
And I can type very long fields,

00:12:09.460 --> 00:12:10.633
but the output of the hash function

00:12:10.633 --> 00:12:13.320
will always be different
and it will always follow

00:12:13.320 --> 00:12:16.400
the same format of having
the very same number

00:12:16.400 --> 00:12:20.523
of character and numbers
in this output format.

00:12:21.550 --> 00:12:24.740
And indeed I can copy whole books into it.

00:12:24.740 --> 00:12:27.739
So this is the book,
"The Catcher in the Rye."

00:12:27.739 --> 00:12:30.670
It consists of over 100
pages as you see here

00:12:30.670 --> 00:12:35.183
and I can just copy this
text and insert it into here.

00:12:35.183 --> 00:12:38.690
And you see it's lots and lots of text,

00:12:38.690 --> 00:12:43.330
and it will give me a unique
hash function output out of it.

00:12:43.330 --> 00:12:47.780
And as a matter of fact, if
I just change one character,

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.090
the whole hash function will change.

00:12:50.090 --> 00:12:52.990
And it will change to
a very significant way,

00:12:52.990 --> 00:12:55.350
it's basically you cannot
recognize it anymore

00:12:55.350 --> 00:12:58.890
just by inserting one space
into the hash functions

00:12:58.890 --> 00:13:00.040
as I'm doing right now.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:02.750
So this is the hash function.

00:13:02.750 --> 00:13:05.700
It transform an input into an output,

00:13:05.700 --> 00:13:07.400
and you can be guaranteed that the output

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:10.213
always stays the same
given a certain input.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:14.400
Now, let's take this understanding

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.883
of what a hash function is
and let's look into blocks.

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:22.394
And blocks are kind of the
main components of blockchains.

00:13:22.394 --> 00:13:24.450
A blockchain are different blocks

00:13:24.450 --> 00:13:26.970
concatenated with each other.

00:13:26.970 --> 00:13:28.340
You will see this in a few minutes,

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:31.400
but let's start with one
individual block right now.

00:13:31.400 --> 00:13:33.170
So this could be this block.

00:13:33.170 --> 00:13:36.330
And a block typically
would have a block number.

00:13:36.330 --> 00:13:38.610
It would have this thing, this nonce,

00:13:38.610 --> 00:13:40.870
we will look into this in a moment,

00:13:40.870 --> 00:13:44.910
and some kind of input field,
and then the hash function.

00:13:44.910 --> 00:13:47.170
And what the hash
function does is it takes

00:13:47.170 --> 00:13:50.580
all these things, the
number, the nonce, the data,

00:13:50.580 --> 00:13:54.270
and then creates a hash
function which is the result

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:57.543
of a kind of all these
inputs taken together.

00:13:58.870 --> 00:14:03.040
And now what we see if is,
if we change something here,

00:14:03.040 --> 00:14:05.330
if we copy it and if we write something

00:14:05.330 --> 00:14:10.330
into the data field, and of
course we can copy it a lot,

00:14:10.370 --> 00:14:12.253
then the hash function changes.

00:14:13.470 --> 00:14:17.620
Now, one key concept of
kind of a blockchain,

00:14:17.620 --> 00:14:20.560
especially prominent in Bitcoin
but many others as well,

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.390
is the concept of mining.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:24.590
And what mining is all about

00:14:24.590 --> 00:14:27.293
is solving a certain cryptographic puzzle.

00:14:28.200 --> 00:14:30.280
And the way this works in this example

00:14:30.280 --> 00:14:34.090
is that here you see the nonce
field, and what we need to do

00:14:34.090 --> 00:14:36.220
in order to successfully solve

00:14:36.220 --> 00:14:37.920
this stylized cryptographic puzzle

00:14:39.280 --> 00:14:41.990
is to change the nonce field in a way

00:14:41.990 --> 00:14:44.910
that the hash function that
resides from that input

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:49.710
and from the other inputs
here starts with four zeros.

00:14:49.710 --> 00:14:52.150
This is how this specific
example is set up.

00:14:52.150 --> 00:14:54.410
Of course, if you look
into other blockchains,

00:14:54.410 --> 00:14:56.270
this will be set up differently.

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:58.380
But here imagine we want to have something

00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:00.623
that starts with four zeros.

00:15:01.500 --> 00:15:03.580
Now, of course, I could
try to do that manually.

00:15:03.580 --> 00:15:07.890
So I could start with one, but with a two,

00:15:07.890 --> 00:15:10.370
now the hash function starts with a C.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:13.480
I could give a three,
it starts with a five.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:15.343
And I can try around a lot,

00:15:16.660 --> 00:15:18.723
and I can try different characters,

00:15:21.630 --> 00:15:23.180
but it's very difficult, right?

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:26.560
Because for me, it's
literally almost impossible

00:15:26.560 --> 00:15:28.440
to guess what the nonce would be

00:15:28.440 --> 00:15:31.190
that would turn the hash
function into a hash function

00:15:31.190 --> 00:15:32.850
that starts with four zeros.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:35.230
And this is now where the mining computers

00:15:35.230 --> 00:15:38.190
or the mining mechanisms come into play.

00:15:38.190 --> 00:15:40.340
And what that is it's basically a computer

00:15:40.340 --> 00:15:41.830
or a kind of a mechanism

00:15:41.830 --> 00:15:44.260
that just in a very short amount of time

00:15:44.260 --> 00:15:47.250
tries lots and lots of
different combinations

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.680
until it has found a combination

00:15:49.680 --> 00:15:51.750
which satisfies the requirement.

00:15:51.750 --> 00:15:53.960
And in this case, the
requirement is that we need

00:15:53.960 --> 00:15:56.160
a hash function that
starts with four zeros.

00:15:57.220 --> 00:15:59.250
So let's try it now, let's click Mine,

00:15:59.250 --> 00:16:01.970
and this will then do it
for us, we calculate a bit,

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:04.467
and now I found a nonce, the 4131.

00:16:05.980 --> 00:16:08.440
And this nonce leads to a hash output

00:16:08.440 --> 00:16:10.980
that starts with four zeros.

00:16:10.980 --> 00:16:12.740
Now just take note that whenever

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:14.743
I just slightly change the nonce,

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:20.200
for instance instead of 4131
which gives us the four zeros,

00:16:20.200 --> 00:16:24.013
I just write 4132 or maybe only 4130.

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:26.840
The four zeros go away.

00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:28.940
In this case, actually
we're lucky we got one zero,

00:16:28.940 --> 00:16:30.857
but of course, one zero isn't enough.

00:16:32.060 --> 00:16:34.440
Let's here satisfy the requirement again.

00:16:34.440 --> 00:16:38.290
And then of course, if I
change here this data field,

00:16:38.290 --> 00:16:39.820
so another input field of the block,

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:42.050
if I just kind of very slightly change

00:16:42.050 --> 00:16:44.930
and just insert an additional character,

00:16:44.930 --> 00:16:47.190
the hash function is invalid as well.

00:16:47.190 --> 00:16:48.823
And I would need to remine it

00:16:49.750 --> 00:16:52.550
in order to get a nonce
combination which would work.

00:16:52.550 --> 00:16:54.850
That might take some
time as you can see here.

00:16:56.680 --> 00:16:58.760
Now it's looking for one
and now this is a new kind

00:16:58.760 --> 00:17:01.110
of combination of a number
that will work here.

00:17:02.950 --> 00:17:04.830
So this is what blocks are.

00:17:04.830 --> 00:17:07.190
You need to kind of have some data inputs.

00:17:07.190 --> 00:17:08.980
And of course, for instance
the data input here

00:17:08.980 --> 00:17:10.360
could be transactions.

00:17:10.360 --> 00:17:13.040
It could be something like the person A

00:17:13.040 --> 00:17:15.753
transferred Bitcoin to person B.

00:17:16.642 --> 00:17:17.870
And this would then the information

00:17:17.870 --> 00:17:19.900
be that is stored in this block.

00:17:19.900 --> 00:17:21.970
And it's secured by the hash function

00:17:21.970 --> 00:17:24.030
because whenever I change something,

00:17:24.030 --> 00:17:25.830
some data in the block,

00:17:25.830 --> 00:17:28.020
then the hash function will become invalid

00:17:28.020 --> 00:17:31.070
according to the certain
requirement that I define.

00:17:31.070 --> 00:17:34.140
And I would need to
recalculate, to remine the block

00:17:34.140 --> 00:17:36.373
in order to kind of make it valid again.

00:17:38.040 --> 00:17:41.150
And this is exactly how
blockchains are secure.

00:17:41.150 --> 00:17:43.170
And to understand that better,

00:17:43.170 --> 00:17:46.650
let's look at another example,
now at a blockchain example.

00:17:46.650 --> 00:17:48.770
And what this is is now a blockchain

00:17:48.770 --> 00:17:50.670
consisting of different blocks.

00:17:50.670 --> 00:17:53.733
And you can see that this
consists of exactly five blocks.

00:17:55.410 --> 00:17:57.000
So how does this work now?

00:17:57.000 --> 00:17:59.670
So let's start with the first block here.

00:17:59.670 --> 00:18:01.410
You see that again we
have the block number,

00:18:01.410 --> 00:18:04.550
we have this nonce field,
and we have the data field.

00:18:04.550 --> 00:18:06.940
The data field is kind
of the body of the block

00:18:06.940 --> 00:18:09.653
where then transaction
data could be inserted.

00:18:10.590 --> 00:18:15.590
And now we have the previous
hash of the previous block.

00:18:16.260 --> 00:18:18.910
And because this is just
the very first block,

00:18:18.910 --> 00:18:21.180
it's just zero.

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:22.120
But then what we have is,

00:18:22.120 --> 00:18:24.310
now this is the hash
for this specific block.

00:18:24.310 --> 00:18:27.080
So what this hash does,
just as we have seen before,

00:18:27.080 --> 00:18:29.500
is it takes all the
inputs here of this block.

00:18:29.500 --> 00:18:31.680
So the block number, the nonce, the data,

00:18:31.680 --> 00:18:33.830
and also this previous hash,

00:18:33.830 --> 00:18:36.760
and just turns it into a new hash.

00:18:36.760 --> 00:18:39.270
And now this block is
linked to the next block,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:40.880
to the block number two

00:18:40.880 --> 00:18:45.130
because this kind of output
hash of the whole block

00:18:45.130 --> 00:18:48.650
is now this previous
hash of the next block.

00:18:48.650 --> 00:18:52.920
So as you can see it's
exactly the same, 0001578.

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:55.390
It's just this very same hash.

00:18:55.390 --> 00:18:57.720
And now again we have the data body

00:18:57.720 --> 00:18:59.200
where we can just insert transactions

00:18:59.200 --> 00:19:00.690
or any other information,

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.960
and now the hash of the block number two

00:19:02.960 --> 00:19:06.000
is computated by taking all these inputs,

00:19:06.000 --> 00:19:08.740
including the hash of the previous block,

00:19:08.740 --> 00:19:10.903
and then calculating a new hash out of it.

00:19:11.870 --> 00:19:13.740
And this way goes on and on, right?

00:19:13.740 --> 00:19:16.730
So this hash, this output
hash of block number two

00:19:16.730 --> 00:19:19.690
is now again an input
into block number three.

00:19:19.690 --> 00:19:21.980
And then the output hash
of block number three

00:19:21.980 --> 00:19:25.220
is calculated taking all the
inputs of block number three,

00:19:25.220 --> 00:19:28.393
including the hash of the previous block.

00:19:29.470 --> 00:19:31.240
And this goes on and on until here,

00:19:31.240 --> 00:19:33.390
the currently last
block of the blockchain.

00:19:34.860 --> 00:19:38.170
Now what happens if we
manipulate this blockchain,

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:41.163
if we kind of insert new data into it?

00:19:42.690 --> 00:19:45.113
So let's write something
like, I'm a hacker.

00:19:45.986 --> 00:19:49.840
So somebody now tried to change the data

00:19:49.840 --> 00:19:51.920
of the information in this blockchain.

00:19:51.920 --> 00:19:55.240
And what you see now is
that this block got unvalid

00:19:55.240 --> 00:19:57.010
because now the new output hash

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:59.590
doesn't start with four zeros anymore.

00:19:59.590 --> 00:20:04.590
So I need to mine it again in
order to make it valid again.

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:07.193
And as you see this
might take a few seconds.

00:20:08.530 --> 00:20:10.750
And indeed, if you now kind of do this

00:20:10.750 --> 00:20:12.370
with the Bitcoin blockchain,

00:20:12.370 --> 00:20:14.390
there it's set up in a
way that mining a block

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:17.200
might take much longer
than only a few seconds.

00:20:17.200 --> 00:20:20.860
It can take you minutes,
hours, maybe even days.

00:20:20.860 --> 00:20:22.740
So just can take you as an individual

00:20:22.740 --> 00:20:25.770
who wants to mine it a
significant amount of time

00:20:25.770 --> 00:20:29.143
until to solve this cryptographic
puzzle that is defined.

00:20:30.800 --> 00:20:32.280
But of course, now what is it

00:20:32.280 --> 00:20:34.960
that really makes the blockchain secure?

00:20:34.960 --> 00:20:37.010
And this is the ability of blockchains

00:20:37.010 --> 00:20:41.380
to make it extremely difficult
to go back to the blockchain

00:20:41.380 --> 00:20:43.860
and change something, some information,

00:20:43.860 --> 00:20:46.250
which is buried deep down in here.

00:20:46.250 --> 00:20:47.900
Because what happens if for instance

00:20:47.900 --> 00:20:51.230
we change information into block three,

00:20:51.230 --> 00:20:53.540
so a block that is kind
of a few blocks down

00:20:53.540 --> 00:20:54.653
in the blockchain.

00:20:55.870 --> 00:20:57.720
So now imagine I'm again a hacker

00:20:57.720 --> 00:21:02.720
and I want to transfer
Thomas 10 coins to Anna.

00:21:05.600 --> 00:21:06.585
Something like that.

00:21:06.585 --> 00:21:09.800
So we want to steal coins from
Thomas and give them Anna.

00:21:09.800 --> 00:21:12.270
The problem now is that not only

00:21:12.270 --> 00:21:14.520
is the block three not valid anymore,

00:21:14.520 --> 00:21:18.480
but all the blocks that follow
block three are invalid.

00:21:18.480 --> 00:21:20.010
And why is that the case?

00:21:20.010 --> 00:21:23.970
Because by changing the data
of this block number three,

00:21:23.970 --> 00:21:27.290
we changed the output hash.

00:21:27.290 --> 00:21:29.750
I mean, if you see here
again, if I do it once more,

00:21:29.750 --> 00:21:31.480
if I just change one character,

00:21:31.480 --> 00:21:33.583
this output hash function changes.

00:21:34.740 --> 00:21:37.760
But then of course I do not
only need to remine this block,

00:21:37.760 --> 00:21:42.360
but this output hash now is also the input

00:21:42.360 --> 00:21:43.500
into the next block.

00:21:43.500 --> 00:21:45.720
So the next block takes this hash function

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:49.000
of block number three
as part of the content

00:21:49.000 --> 00:21:50.663
of the new block number four.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.990
So if we change block number three,

00:21:53.990 --> 00:21:57.380
the hash function of block
number four changes as well

00:21:57.380 --> 00:22:00.140
because it takes the hash
function of number three,

00:22:00.140 --> 00:22:03.003
block number three, the
previous block as an input.

00:22:03.980 --> 00:22:07.540
And again, this output hash of block four

00:22:07.540 --> 00:22:10.820
is again a data input for block five.

00:22:10.820 --> 00:22:13.640
And then the output hash of
block five changes as well

00:22:13.640 --> 00:22:15.870
because this hash changed.

00:22:15.870 --> 00:22:17.380
So what you would need to do in order

00:22:17.380 --> 00:22:19.470
not to manipulate the blockchain

00:22:19.470 --> 00:22:21.730
is to remine all the individual blocks

00:22:21.730 --> 00:22:23.610
starting from the first
that you manipulated.

00:22:23.610 --> 00:22:25.640
So starting with number three.

00:22:25.640 --> 00:22:27.543
And now we mine number four.

00:22:28.950 --> 00:22:30.613
And now we mine number five.

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:34.880
So you literally need to
change the complete blockchain,

00:22:34.880 --> 00:22:36.560
every single block since the block

00:22:36.560 --> 00:22:40.050
that you manipulated in
order to make that happen,

00:22:40.050 --> 00:22:42.590
in order to manipulate the blockchain

00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:44.820
in a way that benefits you.

00:22:44.820 --> 00:22:47.943
And of course,
cryptocurrencies like Bitcoin

00:22:47.943 --> 00:22:49.620
make it extremely difficult for you

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:52.300
because as an individual,
it's just very difficult

00:22:52.300 --> 00:22:55.900
to remine all these individual blocks.

00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:58.770
And especially then also
having all these other nodes

00:22:58.770 --> 00:23:01.890
having saved the former state
of the block within them.

00:23:01.890 --> 00:23:04.513
It's almost impossible to do that.

00:23:06.320 --> 00:23:08.280
So I hope this gives you
some kind of intuition

00:23:08.280 --> 00:23:10.650
into how blockchains work.

00:23:10.650 --> 00:23:14.310
Just to remind you again, this
was a stylized blockchain.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.840
This is not Bitcoin itself

00:23:16.840 --> 00:23:18.630
or this is not any other cryptocurrency

00:23:18.630 --> 00:23:20.950
or any other specific
blockchain that you know.

00:23:20.950 --> 00:23:25.540
This is just a stylized kind
of way how blockchains work.

00:23:25.540 --> 00:23:28.250
But trust me that something like Bitcoin

00:23:28.250 --> 00:23:30.070
works similar to this.

